PAGES=[page('066_page_1.jpg',123,[
 heading('Example 26'),paragraph('Draw the function f(x) = x³ − 3x² − 9x + 1 and its derivative f′(x) = 3x² − 6x − 9. Use these graphs to tell about: (a) When f′(x) is positive, what does that mean in terms of the graph of f(x)? (b) When the graph of f(x) is decreasing, what does that mean in terms of the graph of f′(x)?'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('The graphs of f(x) = x³ − 3x² − 9x + 1 and f′(x) = 3x² − 6x − 9 are shown in Figure 4.8.'),FIG48,
 paragraph('The graphs develop the idea that the critical values of f(x) are always intercepts for the graph of f′(x) = 3x² − 6x − 9: If f′(x) is positive, f(x) is increasing. If f′(x) is negative, f(x) is decreasing.'),note('For this polynomial the critical values are derivative zeros, so they are x-intercepts of f′. In general a critical point can also have an undefined derivative, and then need not be an intercept of a derivative graph.'),
 heading('4.4.4 State the second derivatives rule to find the extreme values of a function at a point'),paragraph('The first derivative of a function can be used to determine whether the function is increasing or decreasing on a given interval. We shall use this information to develop a procedure called the first derivative test for classifying a given point as a relative maximum, a relative minimum, or neither.'),
 paragraph('The steps involved in first-derivative test for relative extrema are: 1. Find all critical values of f(x), that is, all c such that f(c) is defined and either f′(c) = 0 or f′(c) does not exist.'),paragraph('2. The point (c,f(c)) is a relative maximum if f′(x) > 0 (rising) for all x in an open interval (a,c) to the left of c, and f′(x) < 0 (falling) for all x in an open interval (c,b) to the right of c.'),paragraph('3. The point (c,f(c)) is a relative minimum if f′(x) < 0 (falling) for all x in (a,c) to the left, and f′(x) > 0 (rising) for all x in (c,b) to the right.'),paragraph('4. The point (c,f(c)) is not an extremum if f′ has the same sign in (a,c) and (c,b) on both sides of c.'),
 paragraph('In light of first-derivative test, the function f(x) = x³ − 3x² − 9x + 1 (Example 25) has critical values −1 and 3. It is increasing for x < −1 and x > 3, and decreasing for −1 < x < 3. The test gives relative maximum 6 at x = −1 and relative minimum −26 at x = 3.'),
 heading('Example 27'),paragraph('Examine f(x) = 2x³ + 3x² − 12x − 5 for relative extrema using first-derivative test.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('The first derivative is:'),eq(r'f′(x)=6x^2+6x-12=6(x+2)(x-1)'),paragraph('Set f′(x) = 0 for critical values:'),eq(r'6(x+2)(x-1)=0\Rightarrow x=-2,1'),paragraph('To test −2,1, use test values −3,0,2. Other choices are possible, but select numbers that make computations easy. This is shown in Figure 4.9.'),FIG49,
 paragraph('Test values −3 and 0 for x = −2:'),eq(r'f′(-3)=6(-3+2)(-3-1)=24>0'),eq(r'f′(0)=6(0+2)(0-1)=-12<0'),paragraph('The proof continues on page 124.')
]),page('066_page_2.jpg',124,[
 heading('Example 27 — continued'),paragraph('The derivative is positive to the left of −2 and negative to the right. Thus x = −2 leads a relative maximum point:'),eq(r'f(-2)=2(-2)^3+3(-2)^2-12(-2)-5=-16+12+24-5=15'),
 paragraph('Test values 0 and 2 for x = 1:'),eq(r'f′(0)=6(0+2)(0-1)=-12'),eq(r'f′(2)=6(2+2)(2-1)=24'),paragraph('The derivative is negative to the left of 1 and positive to the right. Thus x = 1 leads a relative minimum point:'),eq(r'f(1)=2(1)+3(1)-12(1)-5=-12'),paragraph('The arrow pattern suggests relative maximum (−2,15) and relative minimum (1,−12).'),
 heading('The Second-Derivative Rule'),paragraph('It is often possible to classify a critical point P(c,f(c)) by examining the sign of f″(c). Specifically, if f′(c) = 0 and f″(c) > 0, there is a horizontal tangent at P and f(x) is concave up nearby: its graph is cupped upward from the horizontal tangent, and we expect a relative minimum (Figure 4.11). Similarly, f′(c) = 0 and f″(c) < 0 give a relative maximum, with the graph cupped downward (Figure 4.12).'),
 diagram('source-inflection-schematic','Figure 4.10'),diagram('source-second-test-pair','Figures 4.11 and 4.12'),
 paragraph('In other words: the point P(c,f(c)) is a relative maximum if the slope f′(c) of the tangent line, from left to right along the curve through P, decreases from positive to zero to negative, and f″(c) is negative. The point is a relative minimum if the slope increases from negative to zero to positive, and f″(c) is positive. These observations lead to the second-derivative test.'),
 heading('Remember — The Second Derivative Rule for Relative Extrema'),paragraph('Let f(x) be a function such that f′(c) = 0 and the second derivative exists on an open interval (a,b) containing c. 1. If f″(c) > 0, there is a relative minimum at x = c, and f(x) is concave up nearby. 2. If f″(c) < 0, there is a relative maximum at x = c, and f(x) is concave down nearby. 3. If f″(c) = 0, the second-derivative test fails and gives no information.'),
 note('The source Example 27 line f(1) abbreviates powers because 1² = 1³ = 1; its value −12 is correct. If f″(c) = 0, an extremum may or may not occur; use a sign test or another justified method.')
]),page('067_page_1.jpg',125,[
 heading('Example 28'),paragraph('Use the second-derivative test to determine whether each critical value of f(x) = 3x⁵ − 5x³ + 2 corresponds to a relative maximum, relative minimum, or neither.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('The first and second derivatives are:'),eq(r'f′(x)=15x^4-15x^2=15x^2(x-1)(x+1)'),eq(r'f″(x)=60x^3-30x=30x(2x^2-1)'),paragraph('Set f′(x) = 0 to obtain critical values 0,1,−1.'),eq(r'f″(0)=0'),paragraph('The critical value 0 declares the failure of second derivative test.'),eq(r'f″(1)=30>0'),paragraph('The critical value 1 leads to a relative minimum:'),eq(r'f(1)=3(1)-5(1)+2=0'),eq(r'f″(-1)=-30<0'),paragraph('The critical value −1 gives a relative maximum:'),eq(r'f(-1)=-3-5(-1)+2=4'),
 paragraph('The second derivative test works only for critical values c that make f′(c) = 0. It does not work when f′(c) does not exist or f″(c) = 0. In both cases use the first derivative test.'),note('At 0 the source second-derivative test is inconclusive. Here f′ = 15x²(x² − 1) is negative on both sides of 0, so 0 is neither a maximum nor a minimum; it is a stationary inflection point.'),
 heading('4.4.6 Solve real-life problems related to extreme values'),
 heading('Example 29'),paragraph(r'A truck burns fuel at the rate \(G(x)=\frac1{200}\left(\frac{800+x^2}{x}\right)\), x > 0 gallons per mile, when traveling x miles per hour on a straight level road. If fuel costs $2 per gallon, find the speed that will produce the minimum total cost for a 1000 mile trip. Find the maximum total cost.'),heading('Solution'),
 paragraph('The total trip cost in dollars is gallons per mile times miles times cost per gallon:'),eq(r'C(x)=\frac1{200}\left(\frac{800+x^2}{x}\right)(1000)(2)=\frac{8000+10x^2}{x}'),paragraph('The independent variable represents speed; only x > 0 makes sense. Thus the domain is (0,∞) with no endpoints to check.'),
 eq(r'C′(x)=\frac{10x^2-8000}{x^2},\quad C″(x)=\frac{16000}{x^3}'),paragraph('Set C′(x) = 0:'),eq(r'10x^2-8000=0\Rightarrow x^2=800\Rightarrow x=\pm28.3\ \hbox{mph}'),paragraph('The only critical number in the domain is x = 28.3. The second derivative there is:'),eq(r'C″(28.3)=\frac{16000}{(28.3)^3}=0.72>0'),paragraph('The second-derivative test gives a minimum. Its cost is:'),eq(r'C(28.3)=\frac{8000+10(28.3)^2}{28.3}=565.69\ \hbox{dollars}'),
 note('The source question’s final sentence asks for a maximum, while its solution computes a minimum. The exact minimizing speed is √800 = 20√2 mph, and minimum cost is 400√2 ≈ $565.6854. There is no finite maximum on x > 0: C(x) = 8000/x + 10x tends to infinity both as x → 0⁺ and x → ∞. The rounded intermediate C″ is about 0.706, so 0.72 is only a rough source approximation.'),
 heading('Example 30'),paragraph('The supporting cable of a pipeline suspension system forms a parabolic arc between the supports, described by y = 0.03125x² − 1.25x. Distances are measured in meters. The origin is at the point where the cable attaches to the left support tower. Where is the low point and how far is it below the attachment point?'),diagram('source-pipeline-cable','Figure 4.13'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('For the low point, first and second derivatives are:'),eq(r'y′=0.0625x-1.25,\quad y″=0.0625'),paragraph('Set y′ = 0:'),eq(r'0.0625x-1.25=0\Rightarrow x=20'),paragraph('Since y″ is positive everywhere, x = 20 gives the minimum value. The proof continues on page 126.')
]),page('067_page_2.jpg',126,[
 heading('Example 30 — continued'),paragraph('The low point occurs 20.0 m to the right of the left tower. Put x = 20 into the function to obtain its vertical position:'),eq(r'y=0.03125(20)^2-1.25(20)=-12.5\ \hbox{m}'),paragraph('Therefore, the low point is 20.0 m to the right of, and 12.5 m below, the point of attachment to the support.'),
 heading('4.4.7 MAPLE command maximize (minimize) to compute maximum (minimum) value of a function'),paragraph('The procedure to use MAPLE command maximize (minimize) to compute the maximum (minimum) value of a function is illustrated in the following example.'),heading('Example 31'),paragraph('Use MAPLE commands to compute: (a) f(x) = cosx; (b) f(x) = x⁴ − 2x² + 3 in the interval [−1,2].'),heading('Solution'),
 heading('a. Command:'),command('maximize(cos(x));','1'),command('minimize(cos(x));','−1'),heading('Context Menu:'),command('cos(x)',None),command('Optimization[Maximize](cos(x))',r'\([1.,[x=5.58237824894110\times10^{-17}]]\)'),paragraph('This result is obtained through right click on the last end of the expression by selecting “Optimization < maximize local” on the context menu.'),command('cos(x)',None),command('Optimization[Minimize](cos(x))','[−1., [x = 3.14159265358977]]'),
 heading('b. Command:'),command('maximize(x^4 - 2*x^2 + 3, x = -1..2);','11'),command('minimize(x^4 - 2*x^2 + 3, x = -1..2);','2'),
 note('The numerical Context Menu values describe a maximum near x = 0 and a minimum near x = π, rather than all cosine extrema. The exact global cosine range is [−1,1]. For the interval polynomial, compare both endpoints and interior critical points: f(−1) = 2,f(0) = 3,f(1) = 2,f(2) = 11. These source outputs are independently verified mathematically, not claimed as executed Maple results.'),
 heading('Exercise 4.3'),paragraph('1. Find the critical values of the given functions and show where each is increasing or decreasing: (a) f(x) = x³ + 3x² + 1; (b) f(x) = x³ + 35x² − 125x − 9.375.'),paragraph(r'2. Find critical values: (a) f(x) = 2x³ − 3x² − 72x + 15; (b) \(f(x)=\frac13x^3-x^2-15x+6\); (c) \(f(x)=6x^{2/3}-4x\); (d) \(f(x)=3x^{4/3}-12x^{1/3}\).'),paragraph('Exercise 4.3 continues on page 127.')
])]
